          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Quiv
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv
            or
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DiGraph
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DiGraph
            is the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quivers"} quivers
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graphs"} directed graphs
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            We can define a quiver to be a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\colon X^{op} \to Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{op}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            and two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s, t\colon 1 \to 0
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphisms"} identity morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Quiv
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv
            as the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %ul
            %li
              objects are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\colon X^{op} \to Set
            %li
              morphisms are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
              between such functors.
          %p
            In other words,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Quiv
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{op}
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %h2#as_a_topos As a topos
          %p
            The category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Quiv
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv = Set^{X^{op}}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functors"} representable functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-, 0), X(-, 1)
            may be thought of as the “generic figures” (generic vertex, generic edge) that occur in directed graphs:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo •
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi e
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-, 0) = \bullet, \qquad X(-, 1) = (x \stackrel{e}{\to} y)
          %p From this picture, we can see that:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-, 0)
              has two subobjects:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-, 1)
              has five subobjects:
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi empty
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi e
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} empty, x, y, (x, y), (x \stackrel{e}{\to} y)
          %p
            The fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Quiv
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv
            is a presheaf topos allows us to use
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limits
            of directed graphs exist, or how to construct the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential
            quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            of all morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
            between two quivers in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Quiv
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Quiv
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv
            is the graph without vertices and edges aka the
            = succeed "." do
              %em empty graph
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            is the graph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            with one vertex and one edge.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} Points
            of an arbitrary graph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            i.e. maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\to Y
            then correpond to
            %em loops
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            whence any loopfree graph with a vertex has no points but is not empty (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neq 0
            ).
          %p
            In the following subsections, more of the structure of the topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Quiv
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quiv
            will be worked out explicitly.
          %h3#Omega_graph The subobject classifier
          %p
            Knowing the subobjects of the representable functors allows us to calculate the structure of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            at the corresponding objects of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{op}
          %ul
            %li
              The set of vertices of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo ,
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(0)=\{\emptyset,\bullet\}
            %li
              The set of edges of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi empty
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi e
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(1)=\{empty, x, y, (x, y), (x \stackrel{e}{\to} y)\}
          %p
            We can visualize
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            as the following quiver with two vertices and five edges:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                          %munderover
                            %mo ⇄
                            %mi x
                            %mi y
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mi empty
                          %mo ↺
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↺
                          %mo ↺
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mi e
                              %mi y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi x
                              %mo ,
                              %mi y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\emptyset &amp; \underoverset{x}{y}{\rightleftarrows} &amp; \bullet \\
                  \\mathllap{empty} \circlearrowleft &amp; &amp; \circlearrowleft \circlearrowleft \mathrlap{x \stackrel{e}{\to} y} \\
                  \&amp; &amp; \mathllap{(x, y)}
                  }
          %p
            where the vertex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
            has one loop labeled “empty”, and the vertex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            has two loops with labels
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y)
            and (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi e
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \stackrel{e}{\to} y
            ).
          %p
            Suppose now that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\subseteq Y
            is a subgraph and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_X \colon Y\to\Omega
            is its characteristic map. On vertices:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_X
              maps vertices of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              not in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_X
              maps vertices in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
          %p
            Thus, a vertex is either contained in a subgraph or not. This is represented in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            by two vertices.
          %p
            For edges, the situation is more complicated since there are five cases, which are represented by five edges in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_X
              maps edges which have neither source nor target in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to the loop at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_X
              maps edges with only the source vertex in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_X
              maps edges with only the target vertex in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_X
              maps edges with both source and target vertices in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_X
              maps edges that are in the subgraph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi e
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \stackrel{e}{\to} y
              ).
          %h3#double_negation (Double) negation
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \colon \Omega \to \Omega
            is defined as the characteristic map of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot \colon 1 \to\Omega
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi im
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo ↺
                    %mi empty
                    %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\bot)= \underoverset{{empty}}{\empty}{\circlearrowleft}
          %p
            sits as a subgraph in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
          %ul
            %li
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
              is not in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi im
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\bot)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
              is,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg
              interchanges the two vertices.
            %li
              Thus, all loops at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
              must go to the loop
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi empty
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {empty}
            %li
              The edge
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi empty
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} empty
              goes to (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi e
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \stackrel{e}{\to} y
              ), since it is fully contained in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi im
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\bot)
            %li
              Now
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              has only its target vertex in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi im
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\bot)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %li
              However,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              has only its source vertex in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi im
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\bot)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            Taking the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of a subobject
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\subseteq Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg X
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            that is classified by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\circ\chi_X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            not in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and all the edges in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            between them.
          %p
            Hence, the result
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg X
            of applying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg
            twice to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\subseteq Y
            amounts to adding to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            all the edges of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            that have source and target in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\subseteq Y
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subobject"} dense
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation topology
            given by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\circ\neg \colon\Omega\to\Omega
            precisely when it contains all vertices of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg X
            all the edges in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            between all the vertices in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            By definition, a quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+object"} separated
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            when, for every other quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            and dense subobject
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi S
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon S\hookrightarrow Y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon S\to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \colon Y\to X
            such that the following diagram commutes:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi S
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi g
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  S &amp; &amp; \\
                  i\downarrow &amp;\searrow &amp;f \\
                  Y &amp;\underset{g}{\to} &amp; X
                  }
          %p
            A separated quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            = succeed "-sheaf" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            always exists.
          %p
            Suppose that a quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has a pair of parallel edges
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w,z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:S\hookrightarrow X
            that is just like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            but has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w,z
            omitted is dense in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi zw
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_{zw}:X\to X
            be the automorphism of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that is just like the identity on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            except that it interchanges
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi zw
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X\circ i=\tau_{zw}\circ i=i
            and one sees that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is not separated.
          %p
            Conversely, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a quiver with at most one edge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\to y
            between any pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y)
            of vertices and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:S\to X
            be a map with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:S\hookrightarrow Y
            is dense in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is a bijection on the vertex sets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:Y\to X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            exists the effect of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            on the vertices is uniquely determined by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            but since in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            there is at most one edge between any pair of vertices the image of any edge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\to b
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            under
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is already fixed: it is the unique edge between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(a)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a sheaf precisely when there exists exactly one edge between any pair of vertices since then arbitrary edges in arbitrary factors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            can be mapped to the appropriate edge in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          #negneg_subcats.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A quiver
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is separated for the double negation topology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
              precisely if there exists at most an edge
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\to b
              between any pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b)
              of vertices.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              = succeed "-sheaf" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\to b
              between any pair
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box
          %p
            The corresponding full subcategories are denoted by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sep
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sep_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)
            , respectively. By generalities, it follows that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sep
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sep_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopos"} quasitopos
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
          %p
            Quivers that have at most one edge between any pair of vertices can be called ‘simple’ with the caveat that contrary to (the usual concept of) a simple graph they are allowed to have loops. Similarly,
            = succeed "-sheaves" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
          %p
            Since the edges of
            = succeed "-separated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            = succeed "-separated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sep
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sep_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)
            are equivalent to the categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bin
            with objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\rho)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a set and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            a binary endorelation on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TotalRel
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TotalRel
            of sets equipped with the total relation. The latter can be identified with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            since morphisms between sets equipped with the total relation behave just like ordinary functions between sets.
          %h3#some_subcategories_and_adjunctions Some subcategories and adjunctions
          %p
            The inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi Sep
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)\hookrightarrow Sep_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)
            is actually an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} essential localization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi u
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Bin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e\dashv u\dashv t:Set\hookrightarrow Bin
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            maps a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\tau_X)
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi τ
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_X
            the total relation on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            is the forgetful functor mapping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\rho)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            maps a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\empty)
          %p
            Similarly,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi i
                  %mi Quiv
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)\overset{i}{\hookrightarrow} Quiv
            is an essential subtopos: if we identify sheafification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            with the functor that maps a quiver to the quiver on the same vertex set with edge set the total relation on the vertex set, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi r
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l\dashv r\dashv i
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l
            forgets the edges of a complete quiver.
          %p
            In particular, it follows then from general properties of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation topology
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh_{\neg\neg}(Quiv)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\dashv 1
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\dashv 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \colon Quiv\to Set
            that maps a quiver to its set of loops. Indeed, the adjoint string
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi\dashv\Delta\dashv\Gamma \colon Quiv\to Set
            that comes with the ‘discrete’ inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            that maps a set to the quiver with vertex set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and edge set precisely one loop for every vertex, is not a localisation since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi\dashv\Delta
            is not a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            fails to preserve products.
          %p
            Furthermore, since it is a general result for presheaf toposes (cf.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#RRZ04"} La Palme Reyes et al. 2004
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            has a right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            precisely if a generic figure has a point and in the case of Quiv neither the generic vertex nor the generic edge contains a loop, we see that the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma:Quiv\to Set
            has no right adjoint.
